4.3 Cost logic
1. Look for a cost range for the product whose start and end dates contain the source (not offset) order date. The end date of the current row is already stored as 9999-12-31.
2. If more than one range matches, take the most recent start date, so only one cost is ever returned (R7).
3. If found: UnitCost = that cost, CostSource = History.
4. If none found but the product has a current standard cost: use it, CostSource = Current.
5. If neither exists: UnitCost = 0, CostSource = Missing.
Why the source date? The cost ranges were loaded with original dates. Comparing them to an offset order date would match nothing.
4.4 Business rules
• Revenue is net of line discounts, excluding tax and freight (decision D3).
• Freight and tax never appear in FactSales (decision D5). They live in FactOrder.
• Online orders legitimately have no sales rep, so SalesPersonKey = -1 is correct for them.
• Do not filter any rows out (no status filter, no date filter). The warehouse holds everything in the source.
